# Identical to packaging/aur/cordial-git/cordial-git.install. Said here as
# well as in the pkgdesc, because a package registry is where a false promise
# costs most: someone installs, launches, gets a dialog they did not expect,
# and files a bug against a maintainer rather than reading a README.

_note() {
  cat <<'MSG'

Cordial ships no Roblox build. On first run it offers to fetch one, and
installs it only if Roblox's own signing certificate signed it.

Roblox publishes no Android binary of its own -- its endpoint answers
supportsAndroidBinaries: false -- so the build comes from a third-party mirror,
or from a copy Sober has already unpacked, or from an APK you point it at:

    ~/.var/app/org.vinegarhq.Sober/data/sober/packages/x86_64/com.roblox.client/

A local copy is preferred when there is one: it costs no request and no bytes,
and it gets the same signature check either way.

Start it with `cordial-shell`, or from your application list. `cordial-run` is
the loader the shell launches and is not what you run by hand.

Cordial is early. You can sign in, load an experience, play with keyboard and
mouse, type into text fields and hear sound. Not working: voice chat, and on
roughly one launch in three a signed-in client reaches the home screen and
freezes -- reopening usually works.
https://github.com/luohoa97/cordial#readme says which claims were measured and
how.

MSG
}

post_install() {
  _note
}

post_upgrade() {
  :
}
